#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079717 :tutorial/content "Whenever we have a fraction in an equation, we apply the multiplication principle to get rid of it. For example, consider the equation below:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+12}{3} =6\n$$\n\nSince the opposite of dividing by $3$ is multiplying by $3$, we apply the multiplication principle and multiply both sides of the equation by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{2x+12}{3} &=6 \\\\ 3 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{2x+12}{3}\\right) &= 3 \\cdot 6\\ \\cancel{3} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{2x+12}{\\cancel{3}}\\right) &= 18 \\\\ 2x+12 &= 18 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe process of removing the fraction is called **clearing the rational expression**. We now have an equation that we can solve using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2x+12 &= 18 \\\\ 2x+12-12 &=18-12 \\\\ 2x &=6 \\ \\dfrac{2x}{2} &=\\dfrac{6}{2} \\\\ x &=3 \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "addac451-9882-5c9f-8aa9-adc3fbd177e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8050 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
